ML_PAGE("l6-prep", "<header class=\"page-header theme-l6\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">CHAPTER 6 · MCQ PREPARATION</span><h2>Chapter 6 MCQ Prep</h2><p>The complete MCQ preparation notes in their original order: 30 multiple-choice questions sit inside the sections they belong to, with the answer, the logic and the exam trap hidden until you reveal them.</p></header><div class=\"study-grid three-col\"><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>30</strong><span>MCQs in the notes</span></div><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>30</strong><span>Exam traps explained</span></div><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>30</strong><span>Difficult-mode questions</span></div></div><div class=\"action-row\"><button class=\"primary-btn bg-l6\" data-quiz=\"6\">Start Chapter 6 simulator</button></div><details class=\"topic-index toc-panel theme-l6\" id=\"l6p-toc\"><summary><strong>MCQ notes</strong><span class=\"toc-count\">2 sections</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><input type=\"search\" class=\"toc-search\" placeholder=\"Filter sections...\" aria-label=\"Filter sections\"><ul class=\"toc-list\"><li class=\"toc-group\">PART A - PRACTICE QUESTIONS</li><li data-full=\"Questions\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l6p-s2\" title=\"Questions\">Practice questions (30)</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">PART B - REVISION AND ERROR DIAGNOSIS</li><li data-full=\"Quick revision and error diagnosis\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l6p-s4\" title=\"Quick revision and error diagnosis\">Quick revision and error diagnosis</button></li></ul></details><details class=\"prep-notes theme-l6\" open><summary><div><strong>Chapter 6 MCQ preparation</strong><small>Exam strategy, practice questions, formula sheets and review - section by section, answers hidden.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"markdown-body compact\"><div class=\"notes-toolbar\"><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"open\">Expand all</button><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"close\">Collapse all</button><span>2 sections · click a heading to open it</span></div><h2>Chapter 6 - Common Stocks: MCQ Practice</h2>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> 30 questions · suggested time: 45 minutes · suggested target: 80%. These are study suggestions, not the lecturer's confirmed exam requirements. Choose the single best answer and attempt the questions before opening the separate answer key.</p>\n<p>The questions move from ownership and return to interpreting corporate actions, valuation, dividends, foreign investing and strategy. In a difficult question, identify <strong>the investor's claim → the relevant cash flows → the risk and constraints → the conclusion supported by the evidence</strong>.</p><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l6p-d1\">PART A - PRACTICE QUESTIONS</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l6p-s2\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Questions</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q1</span><div><p>A corporation is liquidated, and its creditors and other senior claimants have been paid in full. Which statement best describes the common shareholder's position?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Common shareholders have a contractual right to a fixed interest payment before creditors.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Common shareholders must receive the original share purchase price.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Common shareholders hold a residual claim on what remains, subject to other senior claims.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A share is a loan with a scheduled maturity date.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Common shareholders are residual owners: senior claims must be satisfied before any remainder is distributed to them.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Ownership is not a promise to repay the share's purchase price.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q2</span><div><p>A share is purchased for RM40, pays RM1 in dividends and ends the year at RM43. Ignoring costs and taxes, what is the one-year holding-period return?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 10.0%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 7.5%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 2.5%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 12.5%</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>HPR = $(43-40+1)/40=4/40=10\\%$. The RM3 price gain and RM1 dividend both contribute.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Leaving out income produces 7.5%.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q3</span><div><p>A share rises from RM25 to RM27 and pays no dividend. An investor says it therefore had no return. Which answer is best?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Correct, because return requires cash dividends.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Incorrect, because the RM2 increase is a capital gain, even though there was no dividend income.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Correct, because a price increase is always a fixed interest payment.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Incorrect, because the firm must have distributed dividends in secret.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The gain is RM2 per share, or $2/25=8\\%$ before costs and taxes, even without dividends.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Income and price appreciation are different sources of return.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q4</span><div><p>A chart shows that a stock index rose over several historical decades. What conclusion is most defensible?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The evidence describes that index and period; future or individual returns may differ and losses are possible.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The historical pattern establishes a guaranteed positive return over the next five years.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Every individual stock rose in every decade.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Stocks contain no inflation or market risk over long periods.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Historical evidence applies to a particular index and period; it does not guarantee future or individual-stock performance.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> An aggregate long-run result is not a promise for a particular holding period.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q5</span><div><p>A market index falls from 1,000 to 780. Under the chapter's simple percentage convention, which description is most appropriate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A 2.2% gain</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A 10% decline, meeting only the chapter's correction threshold</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A 28.2% decline from the initial level</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A 22% decline, meeting the chapter's bear-market threshold</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Decline = $(1{,}000-780)/1{,}000=22\\%$. It meets the slide's 20% bear-market convention.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Use the starting level as the denominator; other definitions may apply in different contexts.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q6</span><div><p>An investor values shares because they offer liquidity and potential returns above some alternatives. Which qualification is most appropriate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> All shares are equally liquid, so transaction costs can be ignored.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Returns may be attractive, but price losses, uncertain dividends and differences in liquidity remain possible.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Common shares promise fixed dividends and principal repayment.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Higher expected return makes every stock suitable for every investor.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Shares offer potential returns and often liquidity, but both the amount of return and the ability to trade at a desired price can vary.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Potential and liquidity are not guarantees.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q7</span><div><p>An existing shareholder receives an opportunity to buy newly issued shares in proportion to existing ownership. This is best described as:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A stock split</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A rights offering</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A dividend payment date</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A market correction</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>A rights offering gives existing holders the opportunity, under its stated terms, to subscribe for new shares.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A split changes share count; it is not the same transaction as issuing shares for new funds.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q8</span><div><p>A shareholder owns 100 of a company's 1,000 shares. The company issues 100 new shares, all bought by other investors. What happens to the shareholder's ownership percentage?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It increases from 10% to 11%.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It falls from 10% to approximately 9.09%.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It stays at 10% because the number of shares held is unchanged.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It becomes 9.09 shares.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Initially $100/1{,}000=10\\%$; afterward $100/1{,}100\\approx9.09\\%$. New shares bought by others dilute the fraction owned.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> An unchanged share count does not imply an unchanged ownership percentage.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q9</span><div><p>A parent company distributes shares in a subsidiary to its existing shareholders, and the subsidiary becomes independent. What has occurred?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A spin-off</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A cash dividend</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A bond maturity</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A rights issue necessarily requiring shareholders to pay cash</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>A spin-off distributes shares in a business that becomes separately owned.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Do not confuse a subsidiary becoming independent with selling subscription rights.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q10</span><div><p>An investor holds 120 shares priced at RM30 each. The company executes a 3-for-1 split. Immediately after the split, assuming no other price change, what is the investor's position?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 40 shares at RM90, worth RM3,600</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 120 shares at RM10, worth RM1,200</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 360 shares at RM30, worth RM10,800</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 360 shares at RM10, worth RM3,600</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Shares become $120\\times3=360$; the adjusted price is $30/3=\\text{RM}10$. Value remains $360\\times10=\\text{RM}3{,}600$, assuming no other change.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Increase shares and adjust price in opposite directions.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q11</span><div><p>Why does a stock split by itself not create economic wealth for existing shareholders?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It makes the company cancel all future dividends.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Shareholders' proportional ownership necessarily doubles.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It automatically raises the company's earnings by the split ratio.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> More shares are normally offset by a proportional adjustment in per-share price, before other market effects.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>With a proportional price adjustment, the value of each shareholder's holding and their ownership percentage are unchanged immediately, absent other effects.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A higher share count alone is not a higher economic claim.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q12</span><div><p>A firm repurchases some of its own outstanding shares and holds them as treasury shares. What is the most direct effect at the transaction date?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> All repurchases guarantee an increase in intrinsic value per share.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The number of shares held by all remaining investors must increase.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The number of shares outstanding to investors decreases.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The firm necessarily pays a cash dividend to every shareholder.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Repurchased shares held in treasury no longer count as outstanding. Their effect on value per remaining share depends on price paid and other circumstances.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A buyback is not automatically value-creating.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q13</span><div><p>Class A and Class B shares belong to the same company but have different voting rights. What follows?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Different classes can give holders different rights, so their terms matter.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Each Class B share must legally have the same voting power as each Class A share.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Both classes must be debt instruments.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Every class must have identical dividend terms in all circumstances.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Classified shares can have different voting or other rights, depending on their actual terms.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Shares in one company need not confer identical privileges.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q14</span><div><p>Under the chapter's stock-trading convention, an order for 75 shares is:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A round lot</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A stock dividend</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> An odd lot</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A rights offering</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The chapter defines less than 100 shares as an odd lot.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Lot labels do not themselves establish a particular transaction fee.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q15</span><div><p>An investor repeatedly trades in and out of the same share while its price ends unchanged. Commissions and spreads are positive. Ignoring taxes and dividends, what is the most likely result?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Each trade increases the gross return automatically.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Trading costs reduce the investor's net return.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The bid-ask spread is income paid to the investor on every trade.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Costs do not matter when the ending price equals the initial price.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Each commission and spread is a trading friction that reduces net performance.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A zero price change does not imply a zero net return after costs.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q16</span><div><p>A company has RM12 million in assets, RM4 million in liabilities, RM2 million in preferred equity and 1 million common shares outstanding. What is its common book value per share under the chapter's convention?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM4</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM8</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM6</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM12</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Common equity = RM12m - RM4m - RM2m = RM6m; book value per common share = RM6m / 1m = <strong>RM6</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Deduct preferred claims under the stated convention before dividing by common shares.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q17</span><div><p>A company's par value is RM1, book value per common share is RM6, market price is RM10 and an analyst estimates intrinsic investment value at RM12. Which statement is correct?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM1 is the company's current market price.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM12 is an estimate of worth; RM10 is the current market price.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM6 is necessarily the price another investor will pay.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> All four measures must be equal.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Market value is the observed trading price; investment value is an estimate of what the stock is worth. Par and accounting book value answer different questions.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> None of these figures must equal another.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q18</span><div><p>Two companies each have 2 million shares outstanding. A's share price is RM5 and B's is RM20. What are their market capitalizations?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A: RM2 million; B: RM20 million</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A: RM40 million; B: RM10 million</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A: RM5 million; B: RM20 million</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A: RM10 million; B: RM40 million</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Market capitalization = outstanding shares x share price. A: $2m\\times5=\\text{RM}10m$; B: $2m\\times20=\\text{RM}40m$.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Share price alone is not total equity market value.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q19</span><div><p>A company reports net income available to common shareholders of RM9 million and has a weighted average of 3 million common shares outstanding. Its earnings per share are:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM0.33</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM12.00</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM6.00</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM3.00</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>EPS = RM9m / 3m shares = <strong>RM3.00 per share</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Divide earnings available to common shareholders by the applicable share count.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q20</span><div><p>A share trades at RM24 and pays RM1.20 per share in annual dividends. What is its indicated dividend yield, assuming the dividend continues at that annual rate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 2%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 20%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 5%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 50%</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Indicated yield = annual dividend / current price = $1.20/24=5\\%$.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> This is current income relative to price, not payout as a fraction of earnings or guaranteed total return.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q21</span><div><p>A company earns RM4 per share and pays RM1.20 per share in annual dividends. What is its dividend payout ratio?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 3.3%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 40%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 30%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 120%</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Payout ratio = dividend per share / EPS = $1.20/4=30\\%$.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Dividend yield uses market price in the denominator; payout uses earnings per share.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q22</span><div><p>A share's annual dividend is unchanged, but its market price falls by 20%. What happens to its indicated dividend yield, all else equal?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It rises because the same dividend is divided by a lower price.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It falls because the company earns a lower price.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It remains unchanged because the dividend is unchanged.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It becomes a guaranteed return.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>If the original price is $P$, the new yield is $D/(0.8P)$, which is 25% higher than $D/P$, assuming the dividend stays unchanged.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A higher yield caused by a falling price does not mean a better investment.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q23</span><div><p>An investor observes a 12% indicated dividend yield after a steep share-price fall. Which response is best?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Buy immediately: the quoted dividend can never be reduced.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Investigate whether the dividend is sustainable and consider the underlying risks and investor's objectives.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Conclude that the investor has already earned 12% during the year.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Treat the dividend yield as identical to total return regardless of price changes.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The dividend could be reduced; investigate earnings, cash flows, risk, price decline and suitability.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> An indicated yield is not a guaranteed realized return.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q24</span><div><p>What does the ex-dividend date generally identify?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The date a company's stock is delisted.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The date on which a company must issue new common shares.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The date on which the dividend is necessarily declared.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The date on or after which a buyer generally does not acquire the right to the declared dividend.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>A buyer on or after the ex-dividend date generally does not receive the declared dividend; settlement and market rules can vary.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Declaration, ex-dividend, record and payment dates have different functions.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q25</span><div><p>Which comparison between a stock dividend and a dividend reinvestment plan (DRIP) is most accurate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A stock dividend provides additional shares; a DRIP applies cash dividends to buy additional shares under its terms.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A stock dividend pays interest on debt; a DRIP retires the underlying shares.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Both guarantee that the investor's wealth rises immediately by the number of shares received.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A DRIP always eliminates investment and transaction risk.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>A stock dividend distributes shares, while a DRIP uses a cash dividend to purchase more shares according to plan terms.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> More shares do not by themselves create guaranteed wealth.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q26</span><div><p>Which example best fits a growth-stock description?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A stock whose dividends are guaranteed by the government.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A company expected to increase operations and earnings rapidly, often retaining much of its earnings.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A company that necessarily has no risk of disappointing investors.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A stock that must also be classified as a bond.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Growth stocks are associated with strong expected growth, often with earnings retained for expansion.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Growth is an expectation, not a guarantee of future performance.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q27</span><div><p>A large, established technology company has a long record of strong earnings and is still growing quickly. Which classification is most defensible?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It can simultaneously be large-cap, technology, growth, and potentially blue-chip.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It cannot be a technology stock because it is large.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It cannot be a growth stock if it has an established record.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Its category labels guarantee a rising share price.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Market capitalization, sector and investment style describe different attributes and can overlap.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> These labels are not mutually exclusive.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q28</span><div><p>An investor expects an economic downturn and compares a cyclical manufacturer with a consumer-staples company. Which assessment is best?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Both stocks must have identical risk because they are equities.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A defensive classification guarantees a positive return in a recession.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The manufacturer is necessarily unaffected by economic conditions.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The staples company may be more defensive, though its price can still fall.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Demand for staples may be more resilient in a slowdown, but company-specific and market risks still exist.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Defensive means relatively resilient, not loss-proof.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q29</span><div><p>A Malaysian investor buys a foreign share for USD100 when the exchange rate is RM4.00 per USD. A year later the share is still USD100, pays no dividend, and the exchange rate is RM4.40 per USD. Ignoring costs and taxes, what is the investor's RM return?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 10%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 0%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> -10%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 40%</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Initial value = $100\\times4.00=\\text{RM}400$; final value = $100\\times4.40=\\text{RM}440$. RM return = $(440-400)/400=10\\%$.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A flat foreign-currency stock price can still produce a home-currency gain or loss.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q30</span><div><p>An investor needs dependable spending money soon and cannot tolerate significant losses. A friend suggests concentrating all the money in a volatile speculative stock because it could double. Which response is most defensible?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Buy it: the possibility of doubling guarantees suitability.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Buy it because frequent trading eliminates market risk.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Assess the investor's liquidity, loss tolerance, diversification and income needs; the concentrated speculative position conflicts with the stated constraints.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Assume the share must distribute a fixed dividend.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>A concentrated speculative position exposes urgently needed funds to significant loss and does not supply dependable income.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Potential upside alone does not establish suitability.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l6p-d3\">PART B - REVISION AND ERROR DIAGNOSIS</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l6p-s4\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Quick revision and error diagnosis</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"l6_yield_payout\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Yield divides the dividend by the share price; payout divides it by earnings per share.</figcaption></figure></div><ul>\n<li><strong>Total holding-period return:</strong> $(\\text{ending price} - \\text{starting price} + \\text{dividends})/\\text{starting price}$, before costs and taxes unless stated.</li>\n<li><strong>Market capitalization:</strong> shares outstanding x current share price. <strong>Common book value per share:</strong> common shareholders' equity / common shares outstanding.</li>\n<li><strong>Dividend yield:</strong> annual dividend per share / share price. <strong>Payout ratio:</strong> dividend per share / EPS. <strong>EPS:</strong> earnings available to common shareholders / relevant weighted-average common shares.</li>\n<li>For an international holding, convert both beginning and ending values into the investor's home currency before computing return, including dividends and stated costs if relevant.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>When reviewing an error, ask whether it arose from the wrong economic claim, the wrong denominator or units, a mistaken transaction, an omitted constraint, or an unsupported guarantee. Then identify what change in the question would make a different answer correct.</p>\n<p><em>Source note:</em> Practice questions and numerical scenarios are original, based on the concepts in Chapter 6, <em>Common Stocks</em> (Pearson slides, 2014). Historical figures, tax statements and named-company classifications in the slides are period-specific; no current tax rule or exam weighting is assumed here.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"question-bank-wrap hard-wrap\" id=\"l6-hard\"><summary><div><strong>Difficult mode - 30 questions</strong><small>18 Bloom-graded questions (3 per level) and 12 marked extension questions. Answers hidden.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><p class=\"hard-intro\">Questions climb Bloom&#39;s taxonomy from <b>Remember</b> to <b>Create</b>. The <b>extension</b> questions go beyond the slides (industry practice, CFA-style, case competitions, Master&#39;s, PhD and postdoc level) but can be reasoned out from the chapter&#39;s principles.</p><div class=\"action-row\"><button class=\"primary-btn\" data-quiz=\"6\" data-quiz-mode=\"difficult\">Start Chapter 6 difficult mode</button><button class=\"secondary-btn\" data-quiz=\"6\" data-quiz-mode=\"extensions\">Extensions only</button></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D1</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>Under the chapter's dividend timeline, which statement describes the <strong>ex-dividend date</strong>?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The date on which the company closes its books and checks its shareholder register to identify every holder entitled to receive the declared dividend.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The date from which a buyer, under the market's settlement rules, generally no longer acquires the right to the declared dividend.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The date on which the cash dividend is actually transferred to shareholders.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The date on which the board announces the amount of the dividend and the other key dates.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>A buyer on or after the ex-date generally does not receive the declared dividend; the seller keeps it. A describes the record date, C the payment date and D the declaration date. The ex-date comes before the record date because trades take time to settle.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D2</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>Under the chapter's convention, which item is subtracted from total assets, together with liabilities, before dividing by the number of common shares to obtain <strong>book value per common share</strong>?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The cost of shares repurchased into treasury during the year</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The company's market capitalisation at the balance-sheet date</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The par value of the common shares outstanding</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Preferred stock</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Book value per common share $= \\dfrac{\\text{assets} - \\text{liabilities} - \\text{preferred stock}}{\\text{common shares}}$. Preferred stock is a claim senior to common equity. Market capitalisation (B) is a market value, not an accounting deduction, and par value (C) is part of common equity, not a claim against it.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D3</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>Measured from a previous peak of a major index, the slides define a <strong>correction</strong> and a <strong>bear market</strong> as declines of at least:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $10\\%$ and $20\\%$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $5\\%$ and $10\\%$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $20\\%$ and $30\\%$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $10\\%$ and $25\\%$</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The slides use three thresholds: a routine decline of $5\\%$ or more, a correction of $10\\%$ or more and a bear market of $20\\%$ or more. B pairs the routine-decline and correction thresholds; C and D are invented. These are descriptive conventions, not forecasts.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D4</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>Why does a $2$-for-$1$ stock split <strong>not by itself</strong> make a shareholder wealthier?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The company must pay for the extra shares out of retained cash, and that payment exactly offsets the value of the new shares received.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The shareholder's ownership percentage halves while the share count doubles, so the two effects cancel.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Shares double and the price roughly halves, so the holding's value and the ownership percentage are unchanged.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> After a split the price per share is legally reset to par value.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>A split cuts the same \"cake\" into more pieces: $N \\times 2$ shares at about $\\dfrac{P}{2}$ each leaves $N \\times P$ unchanged. No cash moves (A is wrong), every holder's shares double so the percentage is unchanged (B is wrong), and par value has nothing to do with the trading price (D).</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D5</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>How can a share's indicated dividend yield <strong>rise</strong> at the same time as the company's prospects <strong>deteriorate</strong>?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The yield divides the dividend by the price; bad news can cut the price while the indicated dividend stays the same.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A falling EPS automatically pushes the yield up, because the yield divides the annual dividend by the earnings per share for the year.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Companies in difficulty are required to raise dividends to keep shareholders.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A deteriorating company retains more earnings, which mechanically increases the yield.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Yield $= \\dfrac{D}{P}$. A smaller denominator raises the ratio with nothing improving in the business. B describes the payout ratio, which divides by EPS. C is false, and retaining more earnings (D) means paying less, which would lower the yield.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D6</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>A European company's depositary shares trade in <strong>ringgit</strong> on a local exchange. What exposure does a Malaysian holder still have?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> None to the euro, because every trade is settled in ringgit.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Only Malaysian market risk, because the shares trade on a Malaysian exchange.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Only the depositary bank's credit risk, because the bank holds the underlying shares and the ringgit quote already removes any currency effect.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Exposure to the European business and to the euro: the ringgit price is the euro price translated at today's rate.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>The trading currency is just the unit of the quote. If the euro weakens against the ringgit, the ringgit price falls even when the euro share price is unchanged. The notes stress that a depositary share quoted in the investor's currency does not remove the underlying foreign business or currency exposure, so A and B are wrong and C names only one minor additional risk.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D7</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p>You buy $300$ shares at $\\text{RM}15.00$, receive two semiannual dividends of $\\text{RM}0.30$ a share, and sell at $\\text{RM}14.10$. Ignoring costs and taxes, what is the holding-period return?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $-6.0\\%$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $-2.0\\%$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $+2.0\\%$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $-4.0\\%$</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Total dividends $= 2 \\times 0.30 = \\text{RM}0.60$; price change $= 14.10 - 15.00 = -\\text{RM}0.90$. $HPR = \\dfrac{0.60 - 0.90}{15.00} = \\dfrac{-0.30}{15} = -2.0\\%$ (a ringgit loss of $300 \\times 0.30 = \\text{RM}90$). A ignores the dividends, D counts only one of them, and C reverses the sign.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D8</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p>A company has $4{,}000{,}000$ shares and you own $200{,}000$ ($5\\%$). It offers $1$ new share for every $5$ held at $\\text{RM}2.50$. Every other holder subscribes in full; you do not. What is your ownership afterwards?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $4.17\\%$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $5.00\\%$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $4.20\\%$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $4.00\\%$</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The others hold $3{,}800{,}000$ shares and buy $\\dfrac{3{,}800{,}000}{5} = 760{,}000$ new ones, so $4{,}760{,}000$ shares are outstanding: $\\dfrac{200{,}000}{4{,}760{,}000} \\approx 4.20\\%$. A wrongly adds your unexercised entitlement to the denominator ($4{,}800{,}000$); B assumes you subscribed; D divides by $5{,}000{,}000$.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D9</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p>Earnings available to common shareholders are $\\text{RM}15$ million, the weighted-average share count is $5$ million, the <strong>quarterly</strong> dividend is $\\text{RM}0.24$ and the share price is $\\text{RM}32$. What are the dividend yield and the payout ratio?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $3.0\\%$ and $32\\%$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $0.75\\%$ and $8\\%$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $32\\%$ and $3.0\\%$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $9.4\\%$ and $32\\%$</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Annual dividend $= 4 \\times 0.24 = \\text{RM}0.96$; $EPS = \\dfrac{15}{5} = \\text{RM}3.00$. Yield $= \\dfrac{0.96}{32} = 3.0\\%$; payout $= \\dfrac{0.96}{3.00} = 32\\%$. B uses the quarterly figure, C swaps the two ratios, and D's $9.4\\%$ is $\\dfrac{EPS}{P} = \\dfrac{3}{32}$, an earnings yield, not a dividend yield.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D10</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>Alpha trades at $\\text{RM}8$ with $50$ million shares outstanding; Beta trades at $\\text{RM}40$ with $8$ million. Which statement is correct?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Beta is the larger company in the stock market because its share price is five times Alpha's.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The two market capitalisations are equal, because Beta's five-times-higher price is exactly offset by Alpha's larger number of shares outstanding.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Alpha's market capitalisation exceeds Beta's by $\\text{RM}320$ million.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Alpha's equity is worth $\\text{RM}80$ million more than Beta's, despite Beta's higher share price.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Alpha: $8 \\times 50\\text{m} = \\text{RM}400$m. Beta: $40 \\times 8\\text{m} = \\text{RM}320$m. Difference $\\text{RM}80$m. The price ratio is $5$ but the share-count ratio is $6.25$, so B is false; C confuses Beta's whole cap with the difference; A judges size by price per share.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D11</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>You hold $1{,}000$ shares at $\\text{RM}50$. The company pays a $10\\%$ <strong>stock</strong> dividend and, immediately afterwards, the shares trade at $\\text{RM}46.00$ with no other news. What is the best interpretation?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> You lost $\\text{RM}4$ a share, so the stock dividend destroyed $\\text{RM}4{,}000$ of value.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Your holding is worth $\\text{RM}50{,}600$; the stock dividend alone would have left it at $\\text{RM}50{,}000$.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Your wealth rose by $10\\%$, because the stock dividend gave you $10\\%$ more shares and each share still represents the same claim on the company.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Your holding is still $1{,}000$ shares, now worth $\\text{RM}46{,}000$.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>You now own $1{,}100$ shares: $1{,}100 \\times 46 = \\text{RM}50{,}600$ versus $1{,}000 \\times 50 = \\text{RM}50{,}000$. With unchanged company value the price would adjust to $\\dfrac{50}{1.10} \\approx \\text{RM}45.45$, which leaves the holding at $\\text{RM}50{,}000$. A ignores the new shares, C treats more shares as more wealth, and D forgets the shares were issued.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D12</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>Year 1: dividend $\\text{RM}1.00$, EPS $\\text{RM}4.00$, price $\\text{RM}25$. Year 2: dividend $\\text{RM}1.00$, EPS $\\text{RM}2.00$, price $\\text{RM}16$. Which statement is correct?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The yield rose but the payout ratio is unchanged, because the payout depends only on the dividend, which stayed at $\\text{RM}1.00$ in both years.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The yield fell and the payout ratio rose.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The yield rose to $6.25\\%$ and the payout to $50\\%$, both because their denominators fell.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Neither ratio changed, because both use the same unchanged dividend.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Yield: $\\dfrac{1}{25} = 4\\%$ to $\\dfrac{1}{16} = 6.25\\%$. Payout: $\\dfrac{1}{4} = 25\\%$ to $\\dfrac{1}{2} = 50\\%$. An unchanged numerator does not mean unchanged ratios (A, D). Both denominators fell, which is why a rising yield and a rising payout together can be a warning sign.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D13</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>A company whose share price fairly reflects its value announces it will buy back $10\\%$ of its shares at a price $30\\%$ <strong>above</strong> the market price. From the viewpoint of a shareholder who does not sell, which assessment is best?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Their percentage rises, but overpaying transfers value to the sellers, so value per remaining share likely falls.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Their wealth must rise, because they now own a larger percentage of the company.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Nothing changes for them, because a buyback only exchanges cash for shares and never affects remaining holders.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Value per share must rise, since the same total earnings are now divided among fewer shares, so EPS increases and the price follows it up.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Illustration: $100$ shares at $\\text{RM}10$ ($\\text{RM}1{,}000$); buying $10$ at $\\text{RM}13$ costs $\\text{RM}130$, leaving $\\dfrac{870}{90} \\approx \\text{RM}9.67$ a share. The notes stress that a larger percentage of a smaller company is not automatically more wealth (B) and that effects depend on the price paid (C). D's EPS argument ignores the cash that left the company.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D14</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>A retiree is drawn to a share yielding $9\\%$ after its price fell $40\\%$. Its payout ratio is $140\\%$ and its debt is rising. Which conclusion is most defensible?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Buy it: the highest yield available is the safest source of retirement income.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Since the payout ratio is above $100\\%$, the company is certain to go bankrupt within the year, so the retiree should sell rather than buy.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The dividend looks unsustainable: payout exceeds earnings and the yield is inflated by the price fall, so investigate further and diversify.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Ignore the payout ratio, because only the dividend yield matters to an income investor.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The yield rose largely because the denominator fell. Paying $140\\%$ of earnings needs cash from reserves or borrowing, and debt is rising: a cut is plausible. The notes warn that a ratio above $100\\%$ warrants examination, not an automatic verdict of failure (so B overreaches), and that yield alone is not a measure of safety (A, D).</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D15</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>An investor argues: \"This share has a par value of $\\text{RM}0.10$ and trades at $\\text{RM}5$, so it is $50$ times overvalued.\" Evaluate the argument.</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Valid: par value is the share's true worth and the market has inflated the price.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Invalid: par is a stated nominal amount, unrelated to fair value; compare the price with an estimate of investment value.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Valid, but only if the book value per share is also $\\text{RM}0.10$.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Invalid, because in an efficient market the price always equals the share's investment value, so no share can ever be described as overvalued.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The slides call par value mainly an accounting term, not very useful to investors. Whether $\\text{RM}5$ is too high depends on an estimate of investment value from expected future benefits and risk. C still compares with the wrong benchmark (book value records accounting amounts), and D wrongly assumes market price and estimated value must coincide.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D16</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>You hold a high-yield share for retirement income. Which monitoring plan best reflects the chapter?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Sell automatically whenever the dividend yield falls below last year's level.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Watch only the dividend yield, and buy more shares whenever it rises, because a higher yield means more income for every ringgit invested at today's price.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> No monitoring is needed, because the company is a well-known blue chip.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Set review triggers: payout beyond earnings and cash flow, rising debt, a dividend cut, or an oversized position; judge total return.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>A good plan defines in advance what evidence would cause a review, covering sustainability (payout, cash, debt), concentration and total return. B is the yield trap: a rising yield often means a falling price. A reacts to the wrong signal, and C treats a label as a guarantee.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D17</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>You want a numerical example showing that a <strong>local gain can become a ringgit loss</strong>. Which pair of returns does it?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Local return $+8\\%$, foreign currency $-10\\%$ against the ringgit</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Local return $+8\\%$, foreign currency $-5\\%$ against the ringgit</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Local return $-8\\%$, foreign currency $+10\\%$ against the ringgit</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Local return $+8\\%$, foreign currency $+10\\%$ against the ringgit</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>$1.08 \\times 0.90 = 0.972$, a ringgit return of $-2.8\\%$ despite the $+8\\%$ local gain. B gives $1.08 \\times 0.95 = 1.026$, still a gain of $2.6\\%$. C shows the reverse case (a local loss turned into a $1.2\\%$ gain), and D gives $+18.8\\%$.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D18</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>Investor A needs dependable cash for living expenses in six months and cannot tolerate significant losses. Which plan best fits the chapter's reasoning?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Put everything in one high-yield blue-chip share, since its dividend will cover the expenses.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Hold the six-month need in liquid, low-risk assets; use shares only for longer-horizon money, after checking dividend sustainability.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Put everything in a diversified basket of growth stocks, because stocks have historically earned more than other assets and diversification removes the risk.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Buy a speculative stock and protect it with a stop-loss order, which guarantees the downside.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The notes say even a high-quality dividend share can fall or cut its dividend at the wrong time, so equities alone may not meet A's liquidity and capital-preservation needs (A). Long-run averages do not help a six-month horizon (C), and a stop-loss is a trigger, not a guaranteed price (D).</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension questions</span><small>beyond the notes, lowest Bloom level first</small></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X2</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Industry</span></div><p>Why do data vendors <strong>split-adjust</strong> historical share prices?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Because splits create value that must be removed from past returns.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Because split adjustment removes the effect of dividends from the price series.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Because a split changes the company's market capitalisation, so earlier prices must be restated to the new, larger equity value of the company.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Otherwise a $2$-for-$1$ split shows as a false $50\\%$ fall; past prices are divided by the split ratio.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>A split changes the unit, not the value, so an unadjusted chart would show a false crash. Splits neither create value (A) nor change market capitalisation (C); dividend adjustment (B) is a separate correction.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X1</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Industry</span></div><p>A company has $10$ million shares at $\\text{RM}6.00$ and announces a $1$-for-$4$ rights issue at $\\text{RM}4.00$. Practitioners compute the <strong>theoretical ex-rights price (TERP)</strong>, the value per share once all rights are exercised. What is it?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $\\text{RM}5.00$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $\\text{RM}6.00$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $\\text{RM}5.60$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $\\text{RM}4.80$</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>$\\text{TERP} = \\dfrac{10\\text{m} \\times 6.00 + 2.5\\text{m} \\times 4.00}{12.5\\text{m}} = \\dfrac{60\\text{m} + 10\\text{m}}{12.5\\text{m}} = \\text{RM}5.60$. A is the simple average of the two prices, ignoring that there are four old shares per new one; B ignores the cheaper new shares. A holder who neither subscribes nor sells the rights loses $\\text{RM}0.40$ a share: the chapter's point that the economic effect depends on the price and on whether rights can be traded.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X3</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · CFA</span></div><p>A Malaysian investor buys $200$ shares at $\\text{EUR}40$ when $\\text{EUR}1 = \\text{RM}5.00$. At year-end each share pays $\\text{EUR}1.20$ and is sold at $\\text{EUR}38$; the dividend and proceeds are converted at $\\text{RM}5.30$. What is the ringgit return?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $+3.88\\%$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $+4.00\\%$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $-2.00\\%$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $+6.00\\%$</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Cost $= 200 \\times 40 \\times 5.00 = \\text{RM}40{,}000$. Ending $= 200 \\times (38 + 1.20) \\times 5.30 = \\text{RM}41{,}552$, so $R = \\dfrac{41{,}552}{40{,}000} - 1 = 3.88\\%$. Check: local $\\dfrac{38 + 1.20 - 40}{40} = -2\\%$, currency $\\dfrac{5.30}{5.00} - 1 = +6\\%$, and $0.98 \\times 1.06 = 1.0388$. B adds the returns, C is the local return only, D the currency return only.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X4</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · CFA</span></div><p>Net income is $\\text{RM}24$m and preferred dividends are $\\text{RM}4$m. The firm starts the year with $8$m common shares and issues $2$m more on 1 July. What is basic EPS?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $\\text{RM}2.40$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $\\text{RM}2.22$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $\\text{RM}2.67$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $\\text{RM}2.00$</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Earnings available to common $= 24 - 4 = \\text{RM}20$m. Weighted-average shares $= 8 + 2 \\times \\dfrac{6}{12} = 9$m. $EPS = \\dfrac{20}{9} \\approx \\text{RM}2.22$. A ignores the preferred dividends and uses year-end shares; C ignores the preferred dividends; D uses the year-end count of $10$m instead of the weighted average.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X5</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Case competition</span></div><p>A firm with $40$m shares at $\\text{RM}5$ (market cap $\\text{RM}200$m) has $\\text{RM}20$m of surplus cash. It can either buy back shares at the market price or pay a special dividend of $\\text{RM}0.50$ a share. Ignoring taxes, costs and signalling, compare the outcomes for an investor holding $4{,}000$ shares.</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The buyback leaves the investor with about $\\text{RM}22{,}222$, because their ownership of the company rises by about $11\\%$ while the share price stays at $\\text{RM}5$.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The dividend leaves the investor with $\\text{RM}22{,}000$: $\\text{RM}20{,}000$ of shares plus $\\text{RM}2{,}000$ of cash.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Both leave wealth at $\\text{RM}20{,}000$: shares at $\\text{RM}5$ after the buyback, or shares at $\\text{RM}4.50$ plus $\\text{RM}2{,}000$ cash.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The buyback is worse, because fewer shares outstanding means the company is worth less per share.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Buyback: $\\dfrac{20\\text{m}}{5} = 4$m shares retired, leaving $\\text{RM}180$m over $36$m shares $= \\text{RM}5$; holding $4{,}000 \\times 5 = \\text{RM}20{,}000$. Dividend: $4{,}000 \\times 0.50 = \\text{RM}2{,}000$ cash, and $\\dfrac{180\\text{m}}{40\\text{m}} = \\text{RM}4.50$ per share, so $18{,}000 + 2{,}000 = \\text{RM}20{,}000$. A treats a larger percentage as more wealth; B forgets the ex-dividend price fall; D ignores that the cash left too.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X7</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Master's</span></div><p>Empirical studies often find that a share price falls on the ex-dividend date by <strong>less</strong> than the dividend. Which explanation is most consistent with the chapter's caution that the drop is only \"approximately\" the dividend?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Taxes and trading costs can make the dividend worth less than its face value, and news also moves the price.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The price actually falls on the payment date, not on the ex-dividend date.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The company replaces the distributed cash on the same day, so its value does not fall.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A cash dividend is paid out of earnings rather than out of the company's assets, so in principle no adjustment to the share price is required at all.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>If dividends are taxed more heavily than capital gains, investors value one ringgit of dividend at less than one ringgit of price, so the equilibrium drop can be smaller. B gets the timing wrong (entitlement ends at the ex-date), and C and D deny that cash leaves the company.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X9</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · PhD</span></div><p>Theory says that, with tradable rights and no frictions, the subscription price of a rights issue does not affect shareholder wealth. Use X1's issue ($\\text{RM}6.00$ share, $1$-for-$4$ at $\\text{RM}4.00$, TERP $\\text{RM}5.60$). A holder of $400$ shares <strong>sells</strong> all $400$ rights at fair value instead of subscribing. What is their position?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Wealth falls to $\\text{RM}2{,}240$, a loss of $\\text{RM}160$.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Still $\\text{RM}2{,}400$ ($\\text{RM}2{,}240$ of shares plus $\\text{RM}160$ cash), with diluted ownership.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Wealth stays at $\\text{RM}2{,}400$ and ownership is unchanged.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Wealth rises to $\\text{RM}2{,}560$, because the proceeds from selling the rights are an extra payment on top of the original value of the holding.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Each right is worth $\\dfrac{5.60 - 4.00}{4} = \\text{RM}0.40$, so $400$ rights fetch $\\text{RM}160$; the shares are worth $400 \\times 5.60 = \\text{RM}2{,}240$; total $\\text{RM}2{,}400 = 400 \\times 6.00$. Wealth is preserved but the holder now owns a smaller fraction (C is wrong). A is the outcome if the rights lapse unsold; D double-counts.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X6</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Case competition</span></div><p>A dual-class IPO gives founders $10$ votes per Class B share. Founders hold $20$m B shares; the public buys $80$m one-vote Class A shares. Which assessment would a careful investor make?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The founders control $20\\%$ of the votes, in line with their $20\\%$ economic stake.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Votes must legally match economic ownership in a listed company, so the ten-vote feature changes nothing about who controls the board.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The founders control exactly half of the votes, so neither side can outvote the other.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Founders hold about $71.4\\%$ of the votes with $20\\%$ of the shares; the public carries most of the risk with little control.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Founder votes $= 20\\text{m} \\times 10 = 200$m; total $= 200\\text{m} + 80\\text{m} = 280$m; $\\dfrac{200}{280} \\approx 71.4\\%$. The chapter's point: ownership percentage alone does not reveal voting control when classes differ, so read the class terms (A, B, C are wrong).</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X8</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Master's</span></div><p>An analyst says: \"Buybacks always raise EPS, and higher EPS always raises the share price, so buybacks always create value.\" Evaluate the claim.</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Correct: a higher EPS always raises the price by the same percentage.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Wrong, because buybacks always reduce EPS by shrinking the company.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Flawed: EPS may rise mechanically, but the cash spent cuts future earnings, and value rises only if the price paid is below value.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Wrong, because repurchased shares held in treasury still count in the EPS denominator, so EPS is unaffected and the price cannot change.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Fewer shares can lift EPS, yet lost interest income or new interest expense reduces earnings, and greater leverage can lower the multiple investors pay. The notes stress that buyback effects depend on the price paid, financing and opportunity cost. Treasury shares are excluded from shares outstanding (D), and B's \"always\" is as wrong as A's.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X10</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · PhD</span></div><p>Figure 6.7 reports average annual stock returns from 1900 to 2011 for markets that existed over the whole period. Why should a researcher hesitate to treat those averages as forecasts?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> More than a century of data from many countries is long enough for each country's average return to be treated as a reliable forecast of its future return.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Countries with higher historical averages will keep outperforming because returns persist.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Arithmetic and compound averages are identical over long periods, so the method is irrelevant.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The sample includes only surviving markets, and long-run averages hide large differences between periods.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Markets that closed or were expropriated drop out of such samples, flattering the averages, and Table 6.1 shows decade returns from about $19\\%$ to about $-1\\%$. The notes insist history is period- and method-specific evidence, not a promise. Arithmetic means exceed compound means when returns vary (C), and persistence (B) is an assumption, not a fact.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X11</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Postdoc</span></div><p>A researcher finds that in an emerging market, local share prices and the local currency tend to fall <strong>together</strong> during crises. What does this imply for a ringgit-based investor?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Ringgit risk exceeds local risk: correlated stock and currency losses compound in crises.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The currency moves offset the stock moves, so ringgit-measured risk is lower than local risk and the investor gains a natural hedge during crises.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Currency risk becomes irrelevant over long horizons, so the finding does not matter.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Because hedging is costly, the currency exposure can be ignored when assessing risk.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>With $1 + R_{\\text{RM}} = (1 + R_{\\text{local}})(1 + R_{\\text{currency}})$, losses on both terms multiply: $0.80 \\times 0.85 = 0.68$, a $32\\%$ ringgit loss from a $20\\%$ local fall. Positive correlation raises variance (B describes negative correlation). The notes also warn that diversification benefits can weaken in stress; C and D dismiss a real exposure.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X12</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Postdoc</span></div><p>Which research design would best test the claim that \"high-dividend-yield stocks are safer\"?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Rank all listed stocks by their current dividend yield and report the average yield of the top tenth, since a higher yield means more dependable income.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Survey investors on whether they feel safer holding high-yield shares.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Sort stocks by yield and compare later total returns, cuts and drawdowns, separating price-driven from dividend-driven yields, with controls.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Pick last year's highest-yield stock and check whether it paid its dividend.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>\"Safer\" must be measured by outcomes (total return, drawdowns, cuts), not by the yield itself (A). Separating the denominator effect from genuine dividend growth isolates the chapter's yield trap, and the controls address confounders. B measures perception, and D is a single anecdote.</p></div></details></div></details></div></div></details>");
